{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787351078714,"sessionID":"ses_fda1daed3ffeZ76o2z0rrfeMgg","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_57244d0ff4aa476fa623952e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/cyr/kernel/abi/ABI.md"},"output":"<path>/home/cyr/kernel/abi/ABI.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # abi/ABI.md — guest-facing interface contracts (v1, FROZEN)\n2: \n3: Binding on both kernel substrate and all services/guests. Changes require a\n4: version bump here + note in MEMORY.md. All integers little-endian (wasm\n5: native). No NUL-terminated strings anywhere: lengths are explicit.\n6: \"Window\" = a region of a session's linear memory the kernel assigns at\n7: instantiation; guests never compute absolute addresses, only window offsets.\n8: \n9: ## 1. Message ports (binding from Phase 4)\n10: \n11: Extra imports appended to the mini-WASI profile (prefix `kern_`):\n12: \n13:     kern_port_create(name_ptr, name_len) -> i32   // own endpoint; -1 on err\n14:     kern_port_bind(name_ptr, name_len)   -> i32   // attach to existing name\n15:     kern_port_send(h, ptr, len)          -> i32   // 0 ok | -2 would-block | -1 err\n16:     kern_port_recv(h, ptr, cap)          -> i32   // >0 len | 0 none | -1 err\n17: \n18: Semantics:\n19: - Datagram style, FIFO per sender→receiver pair, kernel-mediated copy only\n20:   (sessions never share memory). Max payload 4096 bytes per message.\n21: - `recv` never blocks; poll with `sched_yield`.\n22: - One name may have many binders (fan-in); a name has exactly one owner.\n23: - Well-known names: `\"console\"` `\"fs\"` `\"net\"` `\"login\"` `\"shell\"`.\n24: \n25: ## 2. Console window (carried from 8-opcode era, unchanged)\n26: \n27:     +0xF000  u64  write: value printed as hex (\"out 0x...\")\n28:     +0xF008  u64  write: low byte emitted as raw char\n29: \n30: Output only. Input arrives via §4.\n31: \n32: ## 3. Block window (RAM-disk backend in Phase 5, virtio-blk backend in Phase 8)\n33: \n34: Layout at window offset 0 (single outstanding request, polled):\n35: \n36:     u32  magic 'BLKW'          u32  blk_size (=512)\n37:     u64  num_blocks            u32  next_req_id (guest increments)\n38:     -- request mailbox (guest writes) --\n39:     u64 op (1=read, 2=write)   u64 lba     u32 count   u64 off (window offset, =count*blk_size)\n40:     -- completion slot (kernel writes) --\n41:     u32 done_req_id            i32 status (0 ok, <0 err)\n42: \n43: Guest: fill request, bump `next_req_id`, poll until `done_req_id` matches,\n44: then touch `off..off+count*blk_size`. Backend swap (RAM↔virtio-blk) is\n45: invisible to guests — same window, same semantics.\n46: \n47: ## 4. Input events (Phase 7)\n48: \n49:     kern_input_recv(ptr, cap) -> i32   // >0 len | 0 none\n50: \n51: Record stream into `ptr`, each record:\n52: \n53:     u8 kind (1=key_down, 2=key_up)   u8 mods (bit0 shift bit1 ctrl bit2 alt)\n54:     u16 codepoint (Unicode; raw scancodes mapped by kernel)\n55: \n56: Delivered ONLY to the focused session. Focus is a kernel-owned attribute;\n57: `kern_focus_set(port_handle)` moves it (login/shell use this after auth).\n58: \n59: ## 5. Timer window (Phase 8)\n60: \n61:     u64 nanos_since_boot        // read-only, monotonic\n62:     u64 deadline_abs            // guest writes; kernel uses for wakeups\n63:     u32 arm (0/1)               // write 1 after setting deadline\n64: \n65: Preemption itself is kernel-internal (scheduler quantum checked in the\n66: timer ISR); guests need no cooperation for it.\n67: \n68: ## 6. Net packet windows (Phase 9)\n69: \n70: Two identically-shaped windows, RX and TX, each:\n71: \n72:     u32 head  (consumer index)   u32 tail (producer index)\n73:     N slots of: u32 len; u8 data[1526]     (N=256, slot stride 1536)\n74: \n75: Producer writes slot at `tail % N`, increments `tail`; consumer mirrors\n76: with `head`. TX drained by kernel shim into virtio-net; RX filled by shim.\n77: L2/L3/L4 parsing lives entirely in `services/net`.\n78: \n79: ## Gate assertion conventions (all phases)\n80: \n81: `make test` targets assert literal substrings on stripped ANSI serial output;\n82: hex values may be zero-padded (`out 0x0000000000000028`). Each phase adds\n83: `make test-pN`. Budget ≥150 s wall clock per run when the kernel halts.\n84: \n85: ## 7. Kernel-owned service ports (binding from Phase 4)\n86: \n87: Three well-known names are served BY THE KERNEL itself (native endpoints;\n88: no extra imports beyond §1 needed — admin tools are ordinary clients):\n89: \n90:     \"registry\"  session & capability control\n91:     \"devman\"    device enumeration\n92:     \"power\"     reboot / poweroff\n93: \n94: Request framing: single §1 datagram `{u16 op, u16 seq, payload}`; replies\n95: carry the same `seq`. Ops:\n96: \n97:     registry: 1=LIST    -> records {u32 sid, u32 uid, u8 state,\n98:                                     char name[16]} per session\n99:               2=CAPS    {u32 sid} -> {u32 n; rec{n u32 cap_id, u64 rights}}\n100:               3=KILL    {u32 sid} -> status      (needs CAP_KILL right)\n101:               4=SPAWN   {char name[16], char path[64], u32 capmask,\n102:                          u16 argc; args bytes} -> {u32 sid}\n103:                                                    (needs CAP_SPAWN right)\n104:     devman:    1=ENUM   -> {u32 n; rec{u32 class(1=block,2=net,3=input,\n105:                                     4=timer,5=console), u32 inst,\n106:                                     u64 win_off}}   (needs CAP_DEVMAN)\n107:     power:     1=REBOOT 2=OFF                 (needs CAP_POWER right)\n108: \n109: Capability bits (u64 mask, enforced by kernel registry):\n110: bit0 KILL, bit1 DEVMAN, bit2 POWER, bit3 FOCUS, bit4 FS_ADMIN, bit5 NET_ADMIN,\n111: bit6 SPAWN. Login maps users→masks; `admin` gets all bits, regular users get\n112: none of 0-2 and scoped others. Unknown op / insufficient bit => status -1,\n113: audited (see §10).\n114: \n115: SPAWN semantics: kernel instantiates the named module from `/boot/modules/`\n116: as a fresh session with exactly the requested capmask (never more than the\n117: caller's own rights allow — privilege escalation rejected). This is how\n118: shells launch programs; there is no fork/exec anywhere in the system.\n119: \n120: ## 8. Device driver model (two-layer rule)\n121: \n122: Layer 1 — native shim (substrate, mechanism): owns real hardware, exposes\n123: exactly ONE class window (§2–§6) per instance. Budget ≤300 LOC/shim.\n124: Lives in `arch/x86_64/dev/` (machine probing) + `core/dev/` (window\n125: plumbing, registry, portable).\n126: Layer 2 — wasm session (policy, optional): consumes class windows/port\n127: names; never touches hardware. fs.wasm, net.wasm are layer-2 consumers.\n128: \n129: Adding new hardware = exactly three steps:\n130:   1. define its class window layout here (version bump),\n131:   2. write the shim implementing that window,\n132:   3. register instance in devman table (+ capability grant template).\n133: No kernel-wide changes permitted.\n134: \n135: Multiple BACKENDS may exist per class (e.g. block: RAM-disk | virtio-blk |\n136: AHCI; net: virtio-net | E1000). Backends are invisible to guests — devman\n137: ENUM exposes only class/instance/window, never the transport. This is what\n138: makes the same disk image portable across QEMU/VirtualBox/VMware (AGENTS.md\n139: Phase 12).\n140: \n141: IRQ policy: v1 fully polled (windows only). v2 (post-Phase-9, ABI bump)\n142: may add `arm` flags + kernel-routed wakeups; never direct guest IRQs.\n143: \n144: ## 9. Reserved device classes (NOT defined yet — v2 material)\n145: \n146: Class IDs reserved so early code doesn't squat them:\n147: \n148:     6 = WLAN      7 = BLUETOOTH      8 = USB-HC      9 = FRAMEBUFFER\n149: \n150: Window layouts undefined on purpose. When defined, expect:\n151: \n152: - BLUETOOTH: HCI transport window (H4 framing over a UART instance, or\n153:   USB interrupt/bulk pipes once class 8 exists). L2CAP/ATT/GATT live in a\n154:   wasm session — Go is fine at that altitude.\n155: - WLAN: offload-module style only (coprocessor/dongle speaks framed\n156:   Ethernet-ish data + small control channel). Full 802.11 MAC/firmware\n157:   loading per-vendor in the SHIM is explicitly out of model scope — it\n158:   breaks the ≤300 LOC shim budget; such vendors need their own budgeted\n159:   exception documented here before any code.\n160: - USB-HC: xHCI event/command rings behind a simplified transfer-request\n161:   mailbox (same shape philosophy as §3). Prerequisite for mainstream\n162:   dongles of every kind.\n163: \n164: ## 10. Audit trail (binding from Phase 4)\n165: \n166: Every rejected §7 op (unknown op, insufficient capability bit) produces an\n167: audit record the kernel relays to \"console\":\n168: \n169:     [audit] sid=<n> uid=<n> op=<name> reason=<cap|op> target=<port>\n170: \n171: v1 destination is serial only; a file sink under /var/log arrives with\n172: post-v1 storage work. Admin tooling for user management (`useradd`,\n173: `passwd`) edits `/etc/users` through fs.wasm using CAP_FS_ADMIN and then\n174: signals \"login\" to reload — no dedicated user server needed.\n\n(End of file - total 174 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# abi/ABI.md — guest-facing interface contracts (v1, FROZEN)\n\nBinding on both kernel substrate and all services/guests. Changes require a\nversion bump here + note in MEMORY.md. All integers little-endian (wasm\nnative). No NUL-terminated strings anywhere: lengths are explicit.\n\"Window\" = a region of a session's linear memory the kernel assigns at\ninstantiation; guests never compute absolute addresses, only window offsets.\n\n## 1. Message ports (binding from Phase 4)\n\nExtra imports appended to the mini-WASI profile (prefix `kern_`):\n\n    kern_port_create(name_ptr, name_len) -> i32   // own endpoint; -1 on err\n    kern_port_bind(name_ptr, name_len)   -> i32   // attach to existing name\n    kern_port_send(h, ptr, len)          -> i32   // 0 ok | -2 would-block | -1 err\n    kern_port_recv(h, ptr, cap)          -> i32   // >0 len | 0 none | -1 err\n\nSemantics:\n- Datagram style, FIFO per sender→receiver pair, kernel-mediated copy only\n  (sessions never share memory). Max payload 4096 bytes per message.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/cyr/kernel/abi/ABI.md","text":"# abi/ABI.md — guest-facing interface contracts (v1, FROZEN)\n\nBinding on both kernel substrate and all services/guests. Changes require a\nversion bump here + note in MEMORY.md. All integers little-endian (wasm\nnative). No NUL-terminated strings anywhere: lengths are explicit.\n\"Window\" = a region of a session's linear memory the kernel assigns at\ninstantiation; guests never compute absolute addresses, only window offsets.\n\n## 1. Message ports (binding from Phase 4)\n\nExtra imports appended to the mini-WASI profile (prefix `kern_`):\n\n    kern_port_create(name_ptr, name_len) -> i32   // own endpoint; -1 on err\n    kern_port_bind(name_ptr, name_len)   -> i32   // attach to existing name\n    kern_port_send(h, ptr, len)          -> i32   // 0 ok | -2 would-block | -1 err\n    kern_port_recv(h, ptr, cap)          -> i32   // >0 len | 0 none | -1 err\n\nSemantics:\n- Datagram style, FIFO per sender→receiver pair, kernel-mediated copy only\n  (sessions never share memory). Max payload 4096 bytes per message.\n- `recv` never blocks; poll with `sched_yield`.\n- One name may have many binders (fan-in); a name has exactly one owner.\n- Well-known names: `\"console\"` `\"fs\"` `\"net\"` `\"login\"` `\"shell\"`.\n\n## 2. Console window (carried from 8-opcode era, unchanged)\n\n    +0xF000  u64  write: value printed as hex (\"out 0x...\")\n    +0xF008  u64  write: low byte emitted as raw char\n\nOutput only. Input arrives via §4.\n\n## 3. Block window (RAM-disk backend in Phase 5, virtio-blk backend in Phase 8)\n\nLayout at window offset 0 (single outstanding request, polled):\n\n    u32  magic 'BLKW'          u32  blk_size (=512)\n    u64  num_blocks            u32  next_req_id (guest increments)\n    -- request mailbox (guest writes) --\n    u64 op (1=read, 2=write)   u64 lba     u32 count   u64 off (window offset, =count*blk_size)\n    -- completion slot (kernel writes) --\n    u32 done_req_id            i32 status (0 ok, <0 err)\n\nGuest: fill request, bump `next_req_id`, poll until `done_req_id` matches,\nthen touch `off..off+count*blk_size`. Backend swap (RAM↔virtio-blk) is\ninvisible to guests — same window, same semantics.\n\n## 4. Input events (Phase 7)\n\n    kern_input_recv(ptr, cap) -> i32   // >0 len | 0 none\n\nRecord stream into `ptr`, each record:\n\n    u8 kind (1=key_down, 2=key_up)   u8 mods (bit0 shift bit1 ctrl bit2 alt)\n    u16 codepoint (Unicode; raw scancodes mapped by kernel)\n\nDelivered ONLY to the focused session. Focus is a kernel-owned attribute;\n`kern_focus_set(port_handle)` moves it (login/shell use this after auth).\n\n## 5. Timer window (Phase 8)\n\n    u64 nanos_since_boot        // read-only, monotonic\n    u64 deadline_abs            // guest writes; kernel uses for wakeups\n    u32 arm (0/1)               // write 1 after setting deadline\n\nPreemption itself is kernel-internal (scheduler quantum checked in the\ntimer ISR); guests need no cooperation for it.\n\n## 6. Net packet windows (Phase 9)\n\nTwo identically-shaped windows, RX and TX, each:\n\n    u32 head  (consumer index)   u32 tail (producer index)\n    N slots of: u32 len; u8 data[1526]     (N=256, slot stride 1536)\n\nProducer writes slot at `tail % N`, increments `tail`; consumer mirrors\nwith `head`. TX drained by kernel shim into virtio-net; RX filled by shim.\nL2/L3/L4 parsing lives entirely in `services/net`.\n\n## Gate assertion conventions (all phases)\n\n`make test` targets assert literal substrings on stripped ANSI serial output;\nhex values may be zero-padded (`out 0x0000000000000028`). Each phase adds\n`make test-pN`. Budget ≥150 s wall clock per run when the kernel halts.\n\n## 7. Kernel-owned service ports (binding from Phase 4)\n\nThree well-known names are served BY THE KERNEL itself (native endpoints;\nno extra imports beyond §1 needed — admin tools are ordinary clients):\n\n    \"registry\"  session & capability control\n    \"devman\"    device enumeration\n    \"power\"     reboot / poweroff\n\nRequest framing: single §1 datagram `{u16 op, u16 seq, payload}`; replies\ncarry the same `seq`. Ops:\n\n    registry: 1=LIST    -> records {u32 sid, u32 uid, u8 state,\n                                    char name[16]} per session\n              2=CAPS    {u32 sid} -> {u32 n; rec{n u32 cap_id, u64 rights}}\n              3=KILL    {u32 sid} -> status      (needs CAP_KILL right)\n              4=SPAWN   {char name[16], char path[64], u32 capmask,\n                         u16 argc; args bytes} -> {u32 sid}\n                                                   (needs CAP_SPAWN right)\n    devman:    1=ENUM   -> {u32 n; rec{u32 class(1=block,2=net,3=input,\n                                    4=timer,5=console), u32 inst,\n                                    u64 win_off}}   (needs CAP_DEVMAN)\n    power:     1=REBOOT 2=OFF                 (needs CAP_POWER right)\n\nCapability bits (u64 mask, enforced by kernel registry):\nbit0 KILL, bit1 DEVMAN, bit2 POWER, bit3 FOCUS, bit4 FS_ADMIN, bit5 NET_ADMIN,\nbit6 SPAWN. Login maps users→masks; `admin` gets all bits, regular users get\nnone of 0-2 and scoped others. Unknown op / insufficient bit => status -1,\naudited (see §10).\n\nSPAWN semantics: kernel instantiates the named module from `/boot/modules/`\nas a fresh session with exactly the requested capmask (never more than the\ncaller's own rights allow — privilege escalation rejected). This is how\nshells launch programs; there is no fork/exec anywhere in the system.\n\n## 8. Device driver model (two-layer rule)\n\nLayer 1 — native shim (substrate, mechanism): owns real hardware, exposes\nexactly ONE class window (§2–§6) per instance. Budget ≤300 LOC/shim.\nLives in `arch/x86_64/dev/` (machine probing) + `core/dev/` (window\nplumbing, registry, portable).\nLayer 2 — wasm session (policy, optional): consumes class windows/port\nnames; never touches hardware. fs.wasm, net.wasm are layer-2 consumers.\n\nAdding new hardware = exactly three steps:\n  1. define its class window layout here (version bump),\n  2. write the shim implementing that window,\n  3. register instance in devman table (+ capability grant template).\nNo kernel-wide changes permitted.\n\nMultiple BACKENDS may exist per class (e.g. block: RAM-disk | virtio-blk |\nAHCI; net: virtio-net | E1000). Backends are invisible to guests — devman\nENUM exposes only class/instance/window, never the transport. This is what\nmakes the same disk image portable across QEMU/VirtualBox/VMware (AGENTS.md\nPhase 12).\n\nIRQ policy: v1 fully polled (windows only). v2 (post-Phase-9, ABI bump)\nmay add `arm` flags + kernel-routed wakeups; never direct guest IRQs.\n\n## 9. Reserved device classes (NOT defined yet — v2 material)\n\nClass IDs reserved so early code doesn't squat them:\n\n    6 = WLAN      7 = BLUETOOTH      8 = USB-HC      9 = FRAMEBUFFER\n\nWindow layouts undefined on purpose. When defined, expect:\n\n- BLUETOOTH: HCI transport window (H4 framing over a UART instance, or\n  USB interrupt/bulk pipes once class 8 exists). L2CAP/ATT/GATT live in a\n  wasm session — Go is fine at that altitude.\n- WLAN: offload-module style only (coprocessor/dongle speaks framed\n  Ethernet-ish data + small control channel). Full 802.11 MAC/firmware\n  loading per-vendor in the SHIM is explicitly out of model scope — it\n  breaks the ≤300 LOC shim budget; such vendors need their own budgeted\n  exception documented here before any code.\n- USB-HC: xHCI event/command rings behind a simplified transfer-request\n  mailbox (same shape philosophy as §3). Prerequisite for mainstream\n  dongles of every kind.\n\n## 10. Audit trail (binding from Phase 4)\n\nEvery rejected §7 op (unknown op, insufficient capability bit) produces an\naudit record the kernel relays to \"console\":\n\n    [audit] sid=<n> uid=<n> op=<name> reason=<cap|op> target=<port>\n\nv1 destination is serial only; a file sink under /var/log arrives with\npost-v1 storage work. Admin tooling for user management (`useradd`,\n`passwd`) edits `/etc/users` through fs.wasm using CAP_FS_ADMIN and then\nsignals \"login\" to reload — no dedicated user server needed.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":174,"totalLines":174,"truncated":false}},"title":"abi/ABI.md","time":{"start":1787351078695,"end":1787351078708}},"id":"prt_0266d171d001gOUC0L0Zn24w2g","sessionID":"ses_fda1daed3ffeZ76o2z0rrfeMgg","messageID":"msg_0266d074e0012Q8j7rgKAx7O1v"}}
